WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:28.130
While everybody has their eyes on North Korea,
another nuclear program still worries those

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.250
in the White House.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:36.000
That is the nuclear program of the Islamic
Republic of Iran.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.719
This country has such a bad reputation that
when we talk about it, it can feel like we’re

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:44.190
talking about one of those places that only
exists in the movies, one of those countries

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.100
&nbsp;dominated by evil leaders who dream of destroying
the world...

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:53.750
Well, there might be some truth to this, but
definitely not as much as some seem to think.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:01:00.780
In fact, Iran is not the most religious, authoritarian
or the most hostile towards women place in

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.760
this part of the world.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.180
Saudi Arabia is much worse... by far.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.979
Dear viewer, Iran is a country of contradictions,
a country whose government claims to work

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:14.750
under a democratic system, but at the same
time, everything is under the control of one

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:26.110
man: Ali Khamenei, the supreme leader.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.710
Even though, at first glance, he isn't as
scary as his predecessor: Ruhollah Khomeini,

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.770
who was scary just to look at…

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.789
The truth is Ali Khamenei can't stand anything
modern.

00:01:34.789 --> 00:01:38.170
For him, modernity might as well have come
from Satan himself.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:45.390
Well... except for nuclear technology, of
course.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:49.740
That explains why in the past few years Iran
has been famous for two things:

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:50.740
1.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.110
Being one of the countries, alongside with
China, where the most people are executed

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:55.730
every year, and…

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:56.730
2.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.610
Wanting to build their own nuclear bomb.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:04.610
However, unlike what happened with the North
Korean crisis, the US and Iran did reach an

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.110
agreement:

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:13.470
In 2015, President Obama lead the Joint Comprehensive
Plan of Action, an agreement to prevent Iran

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.500
from obtaining a Nuclear Bomb, and an agreement
that many have considered to be one of the

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:26.940
greatest achievements in foreign policy.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:34.450
Although, to be fair, all signs seem to point
to the conclusion that this agreement has

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.579
not kept either party satisfied.

00:02:37.579 --> 00:02:43.239
The President of the United States, Donald
Trump, is pretty clear about his opinion on

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:47.879
the deal: ever since he was a candidate, he
has described it as “the worst agreement

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:49.160
ever negotiated”.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:53.590
During his campaign, Trump vowed on many,
many occasions that his top priority would

00:02:53.590 --> 00:03:15.520
be to put an end to such a catastrophic agreement.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:20.170
And don´t think it was the typical campaign
message that every politician repeats over

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.510
and over again but then soon forgets after
election, not at all in fact.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:46.180
During, in his first speech at the UN Assembly,
Trump repeated it, loud and clear:

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.120
They haven’t done so yet, but it is true
that both the president and many other members

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.930
of the Republican Party loathe, yep, loathe
this agreement.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:04:06.700
And they do because, among other things, agreements
were also signed with North Korea…

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:14.459
And these agreements did not work at all…
except for allowing Pyongyang to lay their

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:15.599
hands on the Bomb.

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:19.850
Well, you might be wondering why it is such
a controversial agreement.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:20.980
We’ll look into that.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:27.920
But before we do, let´s take a look at the
context in which it was signed.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:35.090
IRAN: A BUDDING SUPERPOWER?

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:41.289
During the Cold War, it was said that the
Russians were so obsessed that they saw a

00:04:41.289 --> 00:04:44.039
Chinese man behind every tree.

00:04:44.039 --> 00:04:50.900
Something similar might be happening between
Iran and the USA:

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:54.919
Since the islamic revolution of Khomeini in
1979, Iran has become one of Washington’s

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:55.919
worst enemies.

00:04:55.919 --> 00:05:01.990
It has accused Tehran of being the first sponsor
of radical and anti-American groups like Hezbollah,

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.510
Hamas or the Taliban.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:07.490
Not to mention the support of the Syrian regime
of Bashar Al Asad.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:09.210
But there is more.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:13.610
Washington knows very well that Iran isn't
just any country…

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.810
Look, it’s huge.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:23.510
Iran has more than 80 million inhabitants
and one of the greatest amounts of mineral

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:24.510
wealth on Earth.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:31.810
And, hey, it’s not just oil and natural
gas, but other minerals like copper, iron,

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:36.139
zinc, uranium, silver...etc.

00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:42.639
But yeah, we all know oil and natural gas
are especially important.

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:48.590
Well, that is why I want to ask this question:
What is the biggest fear of many analysts

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:49.590
in Washington?

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:53.200
Well, that is the sum of Iraq and Iran.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:54.550
Let me explain.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:06:00.060
Both countries are neighbours, have a Shiite
majority, and the influence of Tehran on Baghdad

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.949
has skyrocketed in the past few years.

00:06:02.949 --> 00:06:08.830
Well, if we add up the oil reserves of both
countries, the amount is one of the largest

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:12.500
in the world, larger than in Saudi Arabia
or Venezuela...

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:18.470
So, coordinated international politics between
these two countries would result in a superpower,

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:26.190
outside the control of Washington, of almost
120 million people and many, many natural

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:27.190
resources.

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:29.930
That would be a remarkable amount of influence…

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:34.250
So then, what would happen if Iran got a hold
of a nuclear bomb?

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:39.500
On the one hand it would consolidate its role
as an international superpower and its influence

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.580
over allies, like Iraq.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:46.360
On the other hand, it would trigger an arms
race with Saudi Arabia and the Emirates, countries

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:51.160
that have already said that they won't be
left behind if Tehran manages to get a nuclear

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.460
bomb.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.479
The result is obvious:

00:06:55.479 --> 00:07:01.340
Tension, risk of proliferation, risk of nuclear
disaster and also, a loss of American leverage

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:02.340
in the area.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.520
It goes without saying that seeing such radical
governments in possession of such powerful

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:07.520
weapons…

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.350
doesn't exactly make anyone feel very safe.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:18.630
Well, this was the situation when the agreement
was reached in 2015.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:19.630
THE DEAL

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:26.360
For years, the United States has tried to
stop the nuclear program with financial penalties…

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.789
but they were useless.

00:07:28.789 --> 00:07:30.760
Did they harm the economy in Iran?

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.830
Very much, but in no way did they stop the
nuclear program…

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:42.400
So… finally, on the 14th of July 2015, the
Nuclear Deal was signed.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:48.110
An agreement with a clear target: to prevent
proliferation and make sure Iran only use

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:50.419
nuclear technology to peaceful ends.

00:07:50.419 --> 00:07:53.389
However, since then, the deal has suffered
harsh attacks.

00:07:53.389 --> 00:07:56.860
We’re going to have a look at some of the
reasons cited by critics of this agreement.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:02.199
But first, I think now is a good time to think
about the circumstances under which this agreement

00:08:02.199 --> 00:08:03.199
was signed.

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:07.111
And the first thing we need to know is that
the nuclear program in Iran is very, very

00:08:07.111 --> 00:08:09.449
advanced.

00:08:09.449 --> 00:08:15.699
Look, according to US intelligence, if the
agreement was to be broken, Iran could develop

00:08:15.699 --> 00:08:19.599
its first nuclear bomb in a matter of months.

00:08:19.599 --> 00:08:25.930
This is the way it is today, and it was even
truer in 2015 when the Iranian program was

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:27.620
working at its fullest capacity.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:31.610
That is why I want to take a moment to think
about the 3 possible outcomes:

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:37.040
War, agreement, or Iran with nuclear capability…

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:44.260
So, stopping the nuclear program with military
intervention would not have been an easy task.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:47.830
It would have resulted in many victims, the
cost for the US would have been prohibitive,

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:52.110
and the US would have had to keep military
intervention in the Middle East for decades.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.470
To compare, it estimated that this would be
the Iraq's war...

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:58.160
multiplied by 5.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:03.529
Because it’s one thing to destroy infrastructure
and a very different thing to eliminate the

00:09:03.529 --> 00:09:06.209
knowledge a country has to make nuclear bombs.

00:09:06.209 --> 00:09:09.639
Obama knew that this scenario was completely
impossible.

00:09:09.639 --> 00:09:13.290
What he wanted was to get away from the Middle
East, and not to get in any more trouble…

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:18.970
involvement in this region has been a chronic
nightmare for the United States.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:24.810
But sure, on the other hand… not doing anything
would have meant that Iran would have gotten

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:28.880
their new toy… and as we have seen, it would
have resulted in an arms race in the region,

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:30.660
which Washington didn't want either.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:33.269
So, an agreement was the only option left.

00:09:33.269 --> 00:09:36.430
“We make a deal and everybody’s happy,
right?”

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:41.700
Mmmm, wait there, because despite that, many
experts, especially Republicans, don't even

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:57.880
want to hear 
about this pact.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:09.930
Are they all just war hawks determined to
impose their ideas by force?

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:14.190
Well, maybe there are some like that… but
the truth is… they also have a point.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:18.290
They say (and they are right) that the US
already signed many agreements with North

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:23.750
Korea and Pyongyang never kept its word, which
is why North Korea is now a nuclear power...

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:27.580
They are convinced that Iran will do the same…
and there is no better evidence of it than

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:31.140
this: Iran has not stopped their ballistic
missile program.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:37.779
“Iran is boosting its missile capabilities
to order to increase the accuracy, preciseness

00:10:37.779 --> 00:10:38.839
and range”.

00:10:38.839 --> 00:10:41.120
Iran will not stop the missile projects”.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.120
Nader Karimi Joni, Irani journalist

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.320
And this program is being developed...with
North Korean technology...by the way.

00:10:45.320 --> 00:11:03.170
So… it seems clear which way a deal with
Iran will go.

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:14.180
Besides, since the pact has a limited duration,
Iran could still put itself in a position

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:18.769
to get its &nbsp;arsenal the moment the deal was
over--and it could be armed in a matter of

00:11:18.769 --> 00:11:19.769
months.

00:11:19.769 --> 00:11:23.639
However, as we have seen, the alternatives
aren't any better… and you know what?

00:11:23.639 --> 00:11:29.010
Iran doesn't necessarily need to go the way
of North Korea.

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:33.180
IRAN, BENEFITS OF THE AGREEMENT

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:38.910
I’m sure many of you are thinking that comparing
North Korea and Iran is easy.

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:42.150
After all both countries are radical dictatorships,
right?

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:43.160
Well, not really.

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:45.800
To be fair...things are a bit different.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.590
Iranian society is much more openminded than
North Korean society,

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:53.680
In Iran, there is a well formed middle class,
there are political debates, and things like

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.010
having a smartphone and access to the internet
are quite common.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:01.720
In fact, most Iranians, especially the younger
generation, are clearly pro-agreement.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:04.730
You see, it’s not just a matter of international
politics.

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:07.590
It’s also a matter of quality of life.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:12.709
The penalties put in place by the international
community were very damaging for Iran.

00:12:12.709 --> 00:12:20.360
Look, in 2013, when the president Ahmadinejad,
the main defender of the agreement, left power,

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:23.199
the economy of Iran was devastated:

00:12:23.199 --> 00:12:27.450
Foreign investment vanished, the unemployment
rate skyrocketed, inflation grew to around

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:31.300
40% and expectations for improvement weren’t
high…

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.790
In short, it was a complete mess...

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:37.140
But thanks to the agreement the penalties
started to disappear and, and since then,

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:38.910
oil exports have tripled, for example.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:44.089
I mean, Iran is not North Korea; they need
the trade and economic advantages the agreement

00:12:44.089 --> 00:12:45.579
has brought.

00:12:45.579 --> 00:12:50.870
“The Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action
will produce the comprehensive lifting of

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:56.970
all UN Security Council sanctions as well
as multilateral and national sanctions related

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:04.399
to Iran’s nuclear programme, including steps
on access in areas of trade, technology, finance,

00:13:04.399 --> 00:13:05.399
and energy”.

00:13:05.399 --> 00:13:06.399
Preface of the deal with Iran.

00:13:06.399 --> 00:13:07.399
http://eeas.europa.eu/archives/docs/statements-eeas/docs/iran_agreement/iran_joint-comprehensive-plan-of-action_en.pdf

00:13:07.399 --> 00:13:09.590
The hope for Obama´s administration was that
with a more prosperous and trade-open Iran,

00:13:09.590 --> 00:13:13.600
the younger, more open minded generation would
be allowed to take power in a few years.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:18.250
However, those who oppose the agreement argue
it is necessary to be more demanding with

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:22.150
Iran, because otherwise it will eventually
become another North Korea, a new nuclear

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:23.150
power.

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:26.589
Anyway, it is not an easy situation as you
can see, but, truth be told, I think it would

00:13:26.589 --> 00:13:31.029
be a mistake to think that any pact is bad
until proven otherwise.

00:13:31.029 --> 00:13:35.880
Having Tehran under pressure might be a good
strategy, but let's be careful not to go too

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.170
far, or we could all lose.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:41.699
So, now it is your turn: If you were the President
of the United States, what would you do deal

00:13:41.699 --> 00:13:42.699
with Iran?

00:13:42.699 --> 00:13:47.140
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

